It changes the game significantly for the majority of players, who
consider tracking things "by hand" to be sufficiently un-fun as to be
not worth doing, and thus, they don't.

These players now have an added feature they would never have otherwise
had; that of stash tracking.  This _will_ change gameplay for them.

Remember, just because something is possible doesn't mean that people are
doing it.  It is eminently _possible_ to take a pen and paper and write
every piece of useful information down during the game; it is also 
eminently true that most players find it so boring as to not do, which 
is why we keep seeing the auto-note, auto-tracker YANIs coming back through.

>> and if NetHack had automatic stash tracking, probably it would need
>> some other gameplay changes.
> For something that doesn't change anything. That makes no sense.

Again, it changes the game for the majority of players.  Now, rather
than stash tracking and item recollection being either a function of 
skill (superior memory) or planning (writing it down), it's no longer in
the player's hands at all -- the game does it for him.

> list from the screen. That's awkward, but the possiblility already  
> exists. Having it automated just saves me the work.

Right.  And it's the work (or lack thereof) that's part of the game.

For example, a commonly rejected YANI is "add warning before hitting a
floating eye or stepping into lava or (insert dangerous thing here)".
Why is it rejected when it's a simple interface change to warn the 
player?

Because making an _interface_ improvement ("warn of danger") changes
the way the actual game is played.  In Nethack, the player must learn 
not to carelessly walk directly into floating eyes, and that counts as
a skill which is useful for ascension.  A player who does not learn this
skill will either find a lot of early amulets of reflection or will die
a lot.

